625, 0.0178985595703125, 0.0113983154296875, -0.042266845703125, 9.953975677490234e-06, -0.030609130859375, 0.011627197265625, -0.0256195068359375, -0.0138092041015625, 0.030120849609375, 0.00241851806640625, -0.006549835205078125, -0.00031256675720214844, 0.0125885009765625, 0.01477813720703125, 0.005512237548828125, -0.044647216796875, -0.007633209228515625, -0.01873779296875, 0.0203399658203125, -0.00010955333709716797, 0.01369476318359375, 0.0160369873046875, -0.02313232421875, 0.00438690185546875, -0.00510406494140625, -0.00690460205078125, 0.00298309326171875, -0.019805908203125, -0.039703369140625, 0.00424957275390625, 0.01177215576171875, -0.0181732177734375, 0.0017423629760742188, 0.0130157470703125, -0.023681640625, 0.015899658203125, -0.0211334228515625, -0.0270843505859375, -0.0310211181640625, 0.0189666748046875, 0.00949859619140625, -0.0175628662109375, -0.003177642822265625, 0.0171661376953125, -0.016143798828125, 0.030426025390625, 0.01015472412109375, -0.00579071044921875, 0.0064544677734375, 0.0019102096557617188, 0.034515380859375, -0.006168365478515625], "payload": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "text": "**Introduction**  \nThe emergence of organized spatiotemporal patterns is a common feature in oscillatory systems, ranging from neural populations to engineered networks. Understanding and tracking these patterns, especially during transient dynamics when the system is not in a steady state, remains a significant challenge.\n\n**Methodology**  \nWe introduce a framework based on spatial ordinal patterns to characterize the spatiotemporal dynamics of oscillatory systems. The method operates directly on the phase of oscillators\u2014meaning the position within their cycle\u2014rather than their amplitude. For each oscillator, we compare its phase to those of its neighbors to determine the local spatial ordering. When two or more neighboring oscillators have phases that are nearly equal (i.e., their phase difference falls below a small, predefined threshold), we assign a special pattern to capture this near-synchrony. This symbolic representation encodes both phase gradients\u2014smooth changes in phase across space\u2014and synchronized clusters\u2014groups of oscillators with similar phases\u2014within a unified framework.\n\nFrom these local patterns, we compute a spatial permutation entropy at each time point. This metric is calculated by determining the frequency of each possible spatial ordinal pattern across the network and then applying the standard entropy formula to these frequencies. A high spatial permutation entropy indicates a diverse set of local patterns (i.e., complex or disordered spatial organization), while a low value reflects more uniform or ordered spatial structure.\n\n**Results**  \nApplying our framework to synthetic oscillator networks, we find that it distinguishes between phase-locked states that have the same overall level of synchronization but differ in their spatial arrangement of phases. For example, two networks may both exhibit high global synchrony, but one may display a smooth phase gradient while the other forms distinct synchronized clusters; our method differentiates these cases. In partially synchronized states, where some oscillators are synchronized and others are not, the spatial permutation entropy captures the resulting diversity in local patterns.\n\nWe also apply the method to resting-state EEG recordings from human volunteers. Here, the framework distinguishes different brain states within individual volunteers, as reflected by changes in the spatial permutation entropy and the distribution of local patterns across the scalp.\n\n**Conclusion**  \nOur approach provides a concise, phase-based symbolic representation that captures both local and global features of spatiotemporal organization in oscillatory systems. By quantifying the diversity of spatial patterns through permutation entropy, the framework enables the detection of transient dynamics and regime transitions as they occur. These results demonstrate the utility of spatial ordinal patterns for analyzing complex oscillatory data in both synthetic and real-world settings."}}